"Homing Configuration (https://linuxcnc.org/docs/2.9/html/config/ini-homing.html)\nciteturn26view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://linuxcnc.org/docs/2.9/html/config/ini-homing.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 246\nL0: # Homing Configuration\nL1: \nL2: Table of Contents\nL3: \nL4: JavaScript must be enabled in your browser to display the table of contents.\nL5: ## 1. Overview\nL6: Homing sets the zero origin of the G53 machine coordinates. Soft limits are defined relative to the machine origin. The soft limits automatically decelerate and stop the axes before they hit the limits switches A properly configured and functioning machine will not move beyond soft(ware) limits and will have the machine origin set as repeatable as the home switch/index mechanism is.\nL7: Linuxcnc can be homed by eye (alignment marks), with switches, with switches and an encoder index, or by using absolute encoders. Homing seems simple enough - just move each joint to a known location, and set LinuxCNC’s internal variables accordingly. However, different machines have different requirements, and homing is actually quite complicated.\nL8: Note  | While it is possible to use LinuxCNC without homing switches/home procedures or limit switches, It defeats the extra security of the soft limits.\nL9: ## 2. Prerequisite\nL10: \nL11: Homing relies on some fundamental machine assumptions.\nL12: \nL13:   * The negative and positive directions are based on cite0†Tool Movement which can be different from the actual machine movement. I.e., on a mill typically the table moves rather then the tool.\nL14: \nL15:   * Everything is referenced from the G53 machine zero origin, the origin can be anywhere (even outside where you can move)\nL16: \nL17:   * The G53 machine zero origin is typically inside the soft limits area but not necessarily.\nL18:   * The homing switch offset sets where the origin is, but even it is referenced from the origin.\nL19: \nL20:   * When using encoder index homing, the home switch offset is calculated from the encoder reference position, after the home switch has been tripped.\nL21: \nL22:   * The negative soft(ware) limits are the most you can move in the negative direction after homing. (but they might not be negative in the absolute sense)\nL23:   * The positive soft(ware) limits are the most you can move in the positive direction after homing. (but they might not be positive in the absolute sense, though it is usual to set it as a positive number)\nL24: \nL25:   * Soft(ware) limits are inside the limit switch area.\nL26: \nL27:   * (Final) Homed Position is inside the soft limit area\nL28:   * (If using switch based homing) the homing switch(es) either utilize the limit switches (shared home / limit switch), or when using a separate home switch, are inside the limit switch area.\nL29:   * If using a separate homing switch, it is possible to start homing on the wrong side of the home switch, which combined with HOME_IGNORE_LIMITS option will lead to a hard crash. You can avoid this by making the home switch toggle its state when the trip dog is on a particular side until it returns passed the trip point again.\nL30: Said another way, the home switch state must represent the position of the dog relative to the switch (ie before or after the switch), and must stay that way even if the dog coasts past the switch in the same direction.\nL31: Note  | While it is possible to use LinuxCNC with the G53 machine origin outside the soft machine limits, if you use G28 or G30 without setting the parameters it goes to the origin by default. This would trip the limit switches before getting to position.\nL32: ## 3. Separate Home Switch Example Layout\nL33: \nL34: This example shows minimum and maximum limit switches with a separate home switch.\nL35: \nL36: cite1†Image: Example Homing Switch layout Figure 1. Demonstrative Separate Switch Layout\nL37: \nL38:   * A is the negative soft limit\nL39: \nL40:   * B is the G53 machine coordinate Origin\nL41: \nL42:   * C is the home switch trip point\nL43: \nL44:   * D is the positive soft limit\nL45: \nL46:   * H is the final home position (HOME) = 0 units\nL47: \nL48:   * The -L and +L are the limit switches trip points\nL49:   * A<->B is the negative soft limits (MIN_LIMITS) = -3 units\nL50: \nL51:   * B<->C is the home_offset (HOME_OFFSET) = -2.3 units\nL52: \nL53:   * B<->D is the positive soft limits (MAX_LIMITS) = 7 units\nL54: \nL55:   * A<->D is the total travel = 10 units\nL56: \nL57:   * The distance between the limit switches and soft limits (-L<->A and D<-+L) is magnified in this example\nL58: \nL59:   * Note that there is distance between the limit switches and actual physical hard contact for coasting after the amplifier is disabled.\nL60: Note  | Homing sets the G53 coordinate system, while the machine origin (zero point) can be anywhere, setting the zero point at the negative soft limit makes all G53 coordinates positive, which is probably easiest to remember. Do this by setting MIN_LIMIT = 0 and make sure MAX_LIMIT is positive.\nL61: ## 4. Shared Limit/Home Switch Example Layout\nL62: \nL63: This example shows a maximum limit switch and a combined minimum limit/home switch.\nL64: \nL65: cite2†Image: Example shared Home Limit Switch layout Figure 2. Demonstrative Shared Switch Layout\nL66: \nL67:   * A is the negative soft limit.\nL68: \nL69:   * B is the G53 machine coordinate Origin.\nL70: \nL71:   * C is the home switch trip point shared with (-L) minimum limit trip.\nL72: \nL73:   * D is the positive soft limit.\nL74: \nL75:   * H is the final home position (HOME) = 3 units.\nL76:   * The -L and +L are the limit switch trip points.\nL77: \nL78:   * A<->B is the negative soft limits (MIN_LIMITS) = 0 units.\nL79: \nL80:   * B<->C is the home_offset (HOME_OFFSET) = -0.7 units.\nL81: \nL82:   * B<->D is the positive soft limits (MAX_LIMITS) 10 units.\nL83: \nL84:   * A<->D is the total travel = 10 units.\nL85: \nL86:   * The distance between the limits switches and soft limits (-L<->A and D<->+L) is magnified in this example.\nL87:   * Note that there is distance between the limit switches and actual physical hard contact for coasting after the amplifier is disabled.\nL88: ## 5. Homing Sequence\nL89: \nL90: There are four possible homing sequences defined by the sign of HOME_SEARCH_VEL and HOME_LATCH_VEL, along with the associated configuration parameters as shown in the following table. Two basic conditions exist, HOME_SEARCH_VEL and HOME_LATCH_VEL are the same sign or they are opposite signs. For a more detailed description of what each configuration parameter does, see the following section.\nL91: \nL92: cite3†Image: Homing Sequences Figure 3. Homing Sequences\nL93: ## 6. Configuration\nL94: \nL95: The following determines exactly how the home sequence behaves. They are defined in an [JOINT_n] section of the INI file.\nL96: \nL97: Homing Type  | HOME_SEARCH_VEL  | HOME_LATCH_VEL  | HOME_USE_INDEX\nL98: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL99: Immediate  | 0  | 0  | NO\nL100: Index-only  | 0  | nonzero  | YES\nL101: Switch-only  | nonzero  | nonzero  | NO\nL102: Switch and Index  | nonzero  | nonzero  | YES\nL103: \nL104: Note  | Any other combinations may result in an error.\nL105: ### 6.1. HOME_SEARCH_VEL\nL106: \nL107: This variable has units of machine-units per second.\nL108: \nL109: The default value is zero. A value of zero causes LinuxCNC to assume that there is no home switch; the search stage of homing is skipped.\nL110: If HOME_SEARCH_VEL is non-zero, then LinuxCNC assumes that there is a home switch. It begins by checking whether the home switch is already tripped. If tripped it backs off the switch at HOME_SEARCH_VEL. The direction of the back-off is opposite the sign of HOME_SEARCH_VEL. Then it searches for the home switch by moving in the direction specified by the sign of HOME_SEARCH_VEL, at a speed determined by its absolute value.\nL111: When the home switch is detected, the joint will stop as fast as possible, but there will always be some overshoot. The amount of overshoot depends on the speed. If it is too high, the joint might overshoot enough to hit a limit switch or crash into the end of travel. On the other hand, if HOME_SEARCH_VEL is too low, homing can take a long time.\nL112: ### 6.2. HOME_LATCH_VEL\nL113: \nL114: This variable has units of machine-units per second.\nL115: Specifies the speed and direction that LinuxCNC uses when it makes its final accurate determination of the home switch (if present) and index pulse location (if present). It will usually be slower than the search velocity to maximize accuracy. If HOME_SEARCH_VEL and HOME_LATCH_VEL have the same sign, then the latch phase is done while moving in the same direction as the search phase.\nL116: (In that case, LinuxCNC first backs off the switch, before moving towards it again at the latch velocity.) If HOME_SEARCH_VEL and HOME_LATCH_VEL have opposite signs, the latch phase is done while moving in the opposite direction from the search phase. That means LinuxCNC will latch the first pulse after it moves off the switch. If HOME_SEARCH_VEL is zero (meaning there is no home switch), and this parameter is nonzero, LinuxCNC goes ahead to the index pulse search.\nL117: If HOME_SEARCH_VEL is non-zero and this parameter is zero, it is an error and the homing operation will fail. The default value is zero.\nL118: ### 6.3. HOME_FINAL_VEL\nL119: \nL120: This variable has units of machine-units per second.\nL121: \nL122: It specifies the speed that LinuxCNC uses when it makes its move from HOME_OFFSET to the HOME position. If the HOME_FINAL_VEL is missing from the INI file, then the maximum joint speed is used to make this move. The value must be a positive number.\nL123: ### 6.4. HOME_IGNORE_LIMITS\nL124: Can hold the values YES / NO. The default value for this parameter is NO. This flag determines whether LinuxCNC will ignore the limit switch input for this joint while homing. This setting will not ignore limit inputs for other joints. If you do not have a separate home switch set this to YES and connect the limit switch signal to the joint home switch input in HAL. LinuxCNC will ignore the limit switch input for this joint while homing.\nL125: To use only one input for all homing and limits you will have to block the limit signals of the joints not homing in HAL and home one joint at a time.\nL126: ### 6.5. HOME_USE_INDEX\nL127: \nL128: Specifies whether or not there is an index pulse. If the flag is true (HOME_USE_INDEX = YES), LinuxCNC will latch on the rising edge of the index pulse. If false, LinuxCNC will latch on either the rising or falling edge of the home switch (depending on the signs of HOME_SEARCH_VEL and HOME_LATCH_VEL). The default value is NO.\nL129: \nL130: Note  | HOME_USE_INDEX requires connections in your HAL file to `joint.n.index-enable` from the `encoder.n.index-enable`.\nL131: ### 6.6. HOME_INDEX_NO_ENCODER_RESET\nL132: \nL133: Default is NO. Use YES if the encoder used for this joint does not reset its counter when an index pulse is detected after assertion of the joint index_enable HAL pin. Applicable only for HOME_USE_INDEX = YES.\nL134: ### 6.7. HOME_OFFSET\nL135: \nL136: This defines the location of the origin zero point of the G53 machine coordinate system. It is the distance (offset), in joint units, from the machine origin to the home switch trip point or index pulse. After detecting the switch trip point/index pulse, LinuxCNC sets the joint coordinate position to HOME_OFFSET, thus defining the origin, which the soft limits references from. The default value is zero.\nL137: Note  | The home switch location, as indicated by the HOME_OFFSET variable, can be inside or outside the soft limits. They will be shared with or inside the hard limit switches.\nL138: ### 6.8. HOME\nL139: The position that the joint will go to upon completion of the homing sequence. After detecting the home switch or home switch then index pulse (depending on configuration), and setting the coordinate of that point to HOME_OFFSET, LinuxCNC makes a move to HOME as the final step of the homing process. The default value is zero. Note that even if this parameter is the same as HOME_OFFSET, the joint will slightly overshoot the latched position as it stops.\nL140: Therefore there will always be a small move at this time (unless HOME_SEARCH_VEL is zero, and the entire search/latch stage was skipped). This final move will be made at the joint’s maximum velocity unless HOME_FINAL_VEL has been set.\nL141: Note  | The distinction between HOME_OFFSET and HOME is that HOME_OFFSET first establishes the origin location and scale on the machine by applying the HOME_OFFSET value to the location where home was found, and then HOME says where the joint should move to on that scale.\nL142: ### 6.9. HOME_IS_SHARED\nL143: \nL144: If there is not a separate home switch input for this joint, but a number of momentary switches wired to the same pin, set this value to 1 to prevent homing from starting if one of the shared switches is already closed. Set this value to 0 to permit homing even if the switch is already closed.\nL145: ### 6.10. HOME_ABSOLUTE_ENCODER\nL146: \nL147: Use for absolute encoders. When a request is made to home the joint, the current joint position is set to the [JOINT_n]HOME_OFFSET value.\nL148: The final move to the [JOINT_n]HOME position is optional according to the HOME_ABSOLUTE_ENCODER setting:\nL149: \nL150:     `HOME_ABSOLUTE_ENCODER = 0 (Default) joint does not use an absolute encoder\nL151:     HOME_ABSOLUTE_ENCODER = 1 Absolute encoder, final move to [JOINT_n]HOME\nL152:     HOME_ABSOLUTE_ENCODER = 2 Absolute encoder, NO final move to [JOINT_n]HOME`\nL153: \nL154: Note  | A HOME_IS_SHARED setting is silently ignored.\nL155: \nL156: Note  | A request to rehome the joint is silently ignored.\nL157: ### 6.11. HOME_SEQUENCE\nL158: \nL159: Used to define a multi-joint homing sequence HOME ALL and enforce homing order (e.g., Z may not be homed if X is not yet homed). A joint may be homed after all joints with a lower (absolute value) HOME_SEQUENCE have already been homed and are at the HOME_OFFSET. If two joints have the same HOME_SEQUENCE, they may be homed at the same time.\nL160: Note  | If HOME_SEQUENCE is not specified then the joint will not be homed by the HOME ALL sequence (but may be homed by individual joint-specific homing commands).\nL161: \nL162: The initial HOME_SEQUENCE number may be 0, 1 (or -1). The absolute value of sequence numbers must increment by one — skipping sequence numbers is not supported. If a sequence number is omitted, HOME ALL homing will stop upon completion of the last valid sequence number.\nL163: Negative HOME_SEQUENCE values indicate that joints in the sequence should synchronize the final move to [JOINT_n]HOME by waiting until all joints in the sequence are ready. If any joint has a negative HOME_SEQUENCE value, then all joints with the same absolute value (positive or negative) of the HOME_SEQUENCE item value will synchronize the final move.\nL164: A negative HOME_SEQUENCE also applies to commands to home a single joint. If the HOME_SEQUENCE value is negative, all joints having the same absolute value of that HOME_SEQUENCE will be homed together with a synchronized final move. If the HOME_SEQUENCE value is zero or positive, a command to home the joint will home only the specified joint.\nL165: Joint mode jogging of joints having a negative HOME_SEQUENCE is disallowed. In common gantry applications, such jogging can lead to misalignment (racking). Note that conventional jogging in world coordinates is always available once a machine is homed.\nL166: \nL167: Examples for a 3 joint system\nL168: \nL169: Two sequences (0,1), no synchronization\nL170: \nL171:     `[JOINT_0]HOME_SEQUENCE = 0\nL172:     [JOINT_1]HOME_SEQUENCE = 1\nL173:     [JOINT_2]HOME_SEQUENCE = 1`\nL174: Two sequences, joints 1 and 2 synchronized\nL175: \nL176:     `[JOINT_0]HOME_SEQUENCE =  0\nL177:     [JOINT_1]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1\nL178:     [JOINT_2]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1`\nL179: \nL180: With mixed positive and negative values, joints 1 and 2 synchronized\nL181: \nL182:     `[JOINT_0]HOME_SEQUENCE =  0\nL183:     [JOINT_1]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1\nL184:     [JOINT_2]HOME_SEQUENCE =  1`\nL185: \nL186: One sequence, no synchronization\nL187: \nL188:     `[JOINT_0]HOME_SEQUENCE =  0\nL189:     [JOINT_1]HOME_SEQUENCE =  0\nL190:     [JOINT_2]HOME_SEQUENCE =  0`\nL191: One sequence, all joints synchronized\nL192: \nL193:     `[JOINT_0]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1\nL194:     [JOINT_1]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1\nL195:     [JOINT_2]HOME_SEQUENCE = -1`\nL196: ### 6.12. VOLATILE_HOME\nL197: \nL198: If this setting is true, this joint becomes unhomed whenever the machine transitions into the OFF state. This is appropriate for any joint that does not maintain position when the joint drive is off. Some stepper drives, especially microstep drives, may need this.\nL199: \nL200: ### 6.13. LOCKING_INDEXER\nL201: \nL202: If this joint is a locking rotary indexer, it will unlock before homing, and lock afterward.\nL203: ### 6.14. Immediate Homing\nL204: \nL205: If a joint does not have home switches or does not have a logical home position like a rotary joint and you want that joint to home at the current position when the \"Home All\" button is pressed in the AXIS GUI, then the following INI entries for that joint are needed.\nL206: \nL207:     `HOME_SEARCH_VEL = 0\nL208:     HOME_LATCH_VEL = 0\nL209:     HOME_USE_INDEX = NO\nL210:     HOME_OFFSET = 0 (Or the home position offset (HOME))\nL211:     HOME_SEQUENCE = 0 (or other valid sequence number)`\nL212: Note  | The default values for unspecified HOME_SEARCH_VEL, HOME_LATCH_VEL, HOME_USE_INDEX, HOME, and HOME_OFFSET are zero, so they may be omitted when requesting immediate homing. A valid HOME_SEQUENCE number should usually be included since omitting a HOME_SEQUENCE eliminates the joint from HOME ALL behavior as noted above.\nL213: ### 6.15. Inhibiting Homing\nL214: \nL215: A HAL pin (motion.homing-inhibit) is provided to disallow homing initiation for both \"Home All\" and individual joint homing.\nL216: \nL217: Some systems take advantage of the provisions for synchronizing final joint homing moves as controlled by negative [JOINT_N]HOME_SEQUENCE= INI file items. By default, the synchronization provisions disallow joint jogging prior to homing in order to prevent joint jogs that could misalign the machine (gantry racking for example).\nL218: System integrator can allow joint jogging prior to homing with HAL logic that switches the [JOINT_N]HOME_SEQUENCE items. This logic should also assert the motion.homing-inhibit pin to ensure that homing is not inadvertently initiated when joint jogging is enabled.\nL219: Example: Synced joints 0,1 using negative sequence (-1) for synchronized homing with a switch (allow_jjog) that selects a positive sequence (1) for individual joint jogging prior to homing (partial HAL code):\nL220: \nL221:     `loadrt mux2           names=home_sequence_mux\nL222:     loadrt conv_float_s32 names=home_sequence_s32\nL223:     setp home_sequence_mux.in0 -1\nL224:     setp home_sequence_mux.in1  1\nL225:     addf home_sequence_mux servo-thread\nL226:     addf home_sequence_s32 servo-thread\nL227:     ...\nL228:     net home_seq_float <= home_sequence_mux.out\nL229:     net home_seq_float => home_sequence_s32.in\nL230:     net home_seq_s32   <= home_sequence_s32.out\nL231:     net home_seq_s32   => ini.0.home_sequence\nL232:     net home_seq_s32   => ini.1.home_sequence\nL233:     ...\nL234:     # allow_jjog: pin created by a virtual panel or hardware switch\nL235:     net hsequence_select <= allow_jjog\nL236:     net hsequence_select => home_sequence_mux.sel\nL237:     net hsequence_select => motion.homing-inhibit`\nL238: Note  | INI HAL pins (like ini.N.home_sequence) are not available until milltask starts so execution of the above HAL commands should be deferred using a postgui HAL file or a delayed [APPLICATION]APP= script.\nL239: \nL240: Note  | Realtime synchronization of joint jogging for multiple joints requires additional HAL connections for the Manual-Pulse-Generator (MPG) type jog pins (`joint.N.enable`, `joint.N.scale`, `joint.N.counts`).\nL241: An example simulation config (gantry_jjog.ini) that demonstrates joint jogging when using negative home sequences is located in the: configs/sim/axis/gantry/ directory.\nL242: \nL243: * * *\nL244: \nL245: Last updated 2026-10-11 09:59:50 UTC\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNIST Special Publication XXX (https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf)\nciteturn26view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 529\nL0@P0: NISTIR 8091\nL1@P0: Predicting Robot System\nL2@P0: Performance by Measuring and\nL3@P0: Composing Subsystem Attributes\nL4@P0: Michael Shneier\nL5@P0: This publication is available free of charge from:\nL6@P0-1: http://dx.doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.8091NISTIR 8091\nL7@P1: Predicting Robot System Performance\nL8@P1: by Measuring and Composing\nL9@P1: Subsystem Attributes\nL10@P1: Michael Shneier\nL11@P1: Intelligent Systems Division\nL12@P1: Engineering Laboratory\nL13@P1: This publication is available free of charge from:\nL14@P1: http://dx.doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.8091\nL15@P1: November 2015\nL16@P1: U.S. Department of Commerce\nL17@P1: Penny Pritzker, Secretary\nL18@P1: National Institute of Standards and Technology\nL19@P1-2: Willie May, Under Secretary of Commerce for Standards and Technology and Director i\nL20@P2: Abstract\nL21@P2: A robotic system typically consists of a number of subsystems, for example for sensing,\nL22@P2: manipulation, and motion. Performance measurements are frequently obtained\nL23@P2: independently for each of the subsystems but, because of conflicting interactions, it may\nL24@P2: be difficult to predict the performance of the whole system from these measures. It is\nL25@P2: often useful to separate the independent subsystem measures from those that interact and\nL26@P2: to trade off performance between interacting subsystems to achieve global performance\nL27@P2: goals. This paper discusses the representation and composition of performance measures\nL28@P2: that aim at achieving such a global understanding of a robot system’s performance.\nL29@P2: Keywords: Manufacturing System; Performance Evaluation; Performance Measures;\nL30@P2: Robotics; Taxonomy.\nL31@P2: Disclaimer: Commercial equipment and materials are identified in order to adequately\nL32@P2: specify certain procedures. In no case does such identification imply recommendation or\nL33@P2: endorsement by the National Institute of Standards and Technology, nor does it imply\nL34@P2: that the materials or equipment identified are necessarily the best available for the\nL35@P2-3: purpose.1\nL36@P3: 1. Introduction\nL37@P3: Robotic workcells are complex systems made up of components that may themselves be\nL38@P3: complex. Setting up a workcell typically requires the expertise of a robot integrator who\nL39@P3: selects the components and designs their interactions to achieve the desired functionality\nL40@P3: and throughput. This process requires a lot of experience and there are few guidelines for\nL41@P3: how to select components. Usually there are many competing criteria that must be taken\nL42@P3: into consideration, including cost, throughput, reliability, ease of setup and use, and\nL43@P3: quality of the products produced. Ideally, the system would be made up of subsystems,\nL44@P3: each of which is the best available within the budget, resulting in a total system that is,\nL45@P3: similarly, as good as can be obtained for the price. Unfortunately, the definition of “best”\nL46@P3: for a subsystem is usually dependent on the role the subsystem plays in the overall\nL47@P3: workcell and there are often conflicts between the requirements of the subsystems that\nL48@P3: may make it impossible to use them in the same system. As a result, it is very difficult to\nL49@P3: develop a principled approach to workcell design and most implementations involve\nL50@P3: tradeoffs simply to get the system to work.\nL51@P3: While many design problems are indeed intractable, it would be very useful to know\nL52@P3: which ones are not and to understand what makes a given problem unsolvable. This\nL53@P3: would enable a more structured approach to workcell design and let tradeoffs be made\nL54@P3: with greater understanding of what the real issues are. Given that high-level system\nL55@P3: performance goals are frequently much less constrained than lower-level goals, this might\nL56@P3: make it easier to achieve an approximation of the ideal solution. This paper proposes an\nL57@P3: approach to design that starts with performance measures for the subsystems and overall\nL58@P3: system performance goals. In principle, it should be possible to decompose the problem\nL59@P3: into one of three classes, from simplest to most difficult:\nL60@P3: – There is no interaction between the subsystems or any interactions do not place\nL61@P3: constraints on the impacted subsystems.\nL62@P3: – The subsystems interact but the constraints induced by the interactions are\nL63@P3: satisfiable.\nL64@P3: – The subsystems interact and the constraints are not satisfiable (note that\nL65@P3: establishing this may not be decidable).\nL66@P3: In the first case, it is easy to build a system by selecting subsystems that meet the overall\nL67@P3: goals such as performance, cost, and reliability. In the second case, it is more difficult\nL68@P3: because selecting a subsystem might place constraints on how the other subsystems may\nL69@P3: be chosen or may bound the parameters required for another subsystem (e.g., by requiring\nL70@P3: a maximum time to complete a step of the task or a minimum quality of fit to prevent\nL71@P3: later jamming). This leads to a combinatorial problem, but one that can be solved either\nL72@P3: analytically or numerically. The last case is not solvable but it may be possible to\nL73@P3: determine what is preventing a solution and develop an approximation, either by\nL74@P3: modifying the overall system goals or by limiting the impact to a defined class of parts\nL75@P3: (e.g., if the system was designed to make a range of parts, perhaps a subset of the range\nL76@P3-4: can still be produced). 2\nL77@P4: The need for establishing global performance measures through composition of local\nL78@P4: measures is not restricted to manufacturing robotics. For example, a recent report from\nL79@P4: the United States Department of Defense Research and Engineering Community of\nL80@P4: Interest on Automation [1] describes the need in the test and evaluation of autonomous\nL81@P4: systems for composable measures of performance, although the report does not suggest\nL82@P4: any specific measures.\nL83@P4: 2. Related Work\nL84@P4: In general, the performance of a robotic system as a whole is measured with respect to\nL85@P4: the task being accomplished or the goals of the company making the products, while the\nL86@P4: performance of each subsystem is measured against more limited and specific criteria.\nL87@P4: There may not be a direct relationship between the overall goals and the individual\nL88@P4: subsystem goals, but a path must be established that links them if it is to be possible to\nL89@P4: predict the overall performance from that of the components. While this is frequently an\nL90@P4: ad hoc process, a number of multi-objective optimization techniques have been applied to\nL91@P4: try to bridge the gap. Other approaches have included using simulations or methods based\nL92@P4: on fuzzy reasoning or genetic algorithms. Much of the literature is focused on the\nL93@P4: problem of job shop scheduling, which involves assigning tasks to a set of machines so as\nL94@P4: to optimize production. While this is not exactly the same problem discussed here, the\nL95@P4: solution approaches are applicable.\nL96@P4: Several researchers have developed simulation-based systems for manufacturing\nL97@P4: planning. In a European Esprit project, Valckenaers, et al. [2] developed a benchmarking\nL98@P4: system that made use of a web-based simulation. It consists of three parts: a testbench\nL99@P4: assistant that interactively helps set up the problem, the emulator that actually carries out\nL100@P4: the simulation and collects statistics, and the performance evaluation component that\nL101@P4: computes a range of measures that can be used to evaluate how well different control\nL102@P4: schemes work. Xing, et al. [3] define a simulation model to solve the multi-objective\nL103@P4: flexible job shop scheduling problem. They developed their simulation using Matlab and\nL104@P4: developed a model consisting of six components: the input component that defines the\nL105@P4: problem and its associated data; the operation assignment component that optimizes the\nL106@P4: assignment of operations to machines; the operation sequencing component that provides\nL107@P4: the flow of operations through machines; the objective evaluation component that creates\nL108@P4: a schedule of operations; the control component that is responsible for flow control and\nL109@P4: ensures that the scheduled operation sequence is feasible; and the output component that\nL110@P4: presents the optimization results to the user. In a different application, that of\nL111@P4: Input/output subsystems in a computer operating system, Ganger and Patt [4] show how a\nL112@P4: simulation enables understanding of subtle issues that arise when performance measures\nL113@P4: of subsystems are rolled up into performance measures for a whole system.\nL114@P4: A system that is designed with goals very much in line with what is proposed here is Shin\nL115@P4: and Park [5]. Their goal is to address engineering problems in which multiple subsystems\nL116@P4: are involved, each of which uses different performance measures. The relationships\nL117@P4: between subsystems are such that inputs for one subsystem are outputs from another. In\nL118@P4: their approach, the system is decomposed into subsystems corresponding to the parts for\nL119@P4-5: which performance values have been defined. An optimization is carried out for each 3\nL120@P5: subsystem using its own design variables, objective function, and constraints. In these\nL121@P5: optimizations, the coupling terms with other subsystems are treated as constants. The\nL122@P5: coupled physics is solved separately and transferred to the individual subsystems and the\nL123@P5: process is iterated until the convergence criteria are satisfied.\nL124@P5: Genetic algorithms are another commonly-used approach to the challenges of combining\nL125@P5: multiple components into a working system that addresses the constraints imposed by\nL126@P5: subsystems. Sivakumar, et al. [6] apply a genetic approach to the problem of tolerance\nL127@P5: allocation across a number of manufacturing subsystems. Here, the goal is to maintain the\nL128@P5: tolerance requirements of parts as they are processed during manufacture. This affects the\nL129@P5: choice of machines to use and processing methods to apply. They use two different\nL130@P5: genetic algorithms to search for optimal solutions and show numerically the success of\nL131@P5: their proposed methods.\nL132@P5: Another genetic algorithm approach is presented by Taboada, et al. [7], who explore two\nL133@P5: problems in flexible manufacturing systems. In the first, a range of products will be made\nL134@P5: and the goal is to select the machines to be included (and the vendors) and to determine\n"
